            <div xml:id="d255">

                <pb facs="d254-1" n="1" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v10-12mSupp/d254-1"/>

                <head>255. Telegram 4455 from Agency personnel in Nicaragua to CIA, April 19<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d255-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Nicaragua</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1961-04-19T04:28:00+00:00">April 19, 1961,
                            0428Z</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <pb facs="d255-1" n="1" type="facsimile" xml:id="d255-1"/>
                <p>CITE [<hi rendition="#italic">less than 1 line not declassified</hi>]</p>
                <p>[<hi rendition="#italic">less than 1 line not declassified</hi>]</p>
                <p>1. Concur, already under way. Two B–26’s schedule out every two hours to work all
                    roads leading into beachhead. C–54 being loaded with 55 gallon drums of napalm.
                    Also to hit beachroads and MSR’s (main supply roads) approaching beach.</p>
                <pb facs="0792" n="617" type="typeset" xml:id="pg617"/>
                <p>2. All above crews will be American. Cuban crews nearly broken without at least
                    12 to 24 hrs off.</p>
                <p>3. Above efforts will continue through tomorrow but very concerned re lack of
                    effective Navy air cover. Tonites B–26 mission were met at the 12 mile limit
                    inbound and outbound. Beach repts enemy aircraft made passes on beach while Navy
                    A/C remained at high altitude. If this procedure used in the morning Hqtrs can
                    expect to lose some American crews.</p>
                <p>End of message</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
